The keyboard should have a separate [Command] and [Control] key, and also
separate keys for [Line Break] vs [Send], and possibly also separate keys
for [Tab] vs [Next Field].

The keyboard manager (through the keyboard capability that a program might
have) would use a 32-bit key code in a keyboard event. The key codes less
than 0x200000 are used for stuff other than TRON characters, and the mask
0x1F0000 will be used for modifier bits. The key codes in range 0x212121
to 0xFFFDFDFD are TRON characters, with no modifier bits. Key codes greater
than or equal to 0xFFFFFE00 are permanently invalid codes, which may be
used in contexts where invalid codes are helpful; specifically, the code
0xFFFFFFFF means the lack of a key code.

How the keyboard input is translated to key codes depends on the
application keyboard mode, and is normally only possible when focused.
Furthermore, some combinations are handled by the window manager, IME,
operating system, etc, in which case they are intercepted and the
application program does not receive those events.

A summary of the possible modes is:
                      Parameter   IME    Release Events   Mod. Events
 -------------------+-----------+------+----------------+-------------+
 Long character     |     -     | Yes  |  No            |  No         |
 Short character    | Code page | Var. |  No            |  No         |
 Command            |     -     | No   |  No            |  No         |
 Game               |     -     | No   |  Yes           |  Yes        |
 Hybrid             | Code page | No   |  Yes           |  Yes        |

* Long character: This mode allows text input with TRON characters. You
can also input control characters, and use non-text characters. IME is
enabled if available.

* Short character: Inputs text with a specified character set of 8-bit
characters. This can be used for situations where ASCII is required or
where a specialized character set (e.g. APL) is required.

* Command: No text input, although you can input ASCII characters without
IME; they are treated as commands rather than as text, so some of the
handling is different, although e.g. [Shift] and [Control] can still be
used to produce different ASCII characters. (

* Game: Other than key combinations reserved for the system, it treats the
keys as a joypad with many buttons, and includes release events. It can
also handle events for modifier keys. However, if [System] is held then no
keyboard events (including that for the [System] key itself) can be sent to
the application program. IME, text entry, etc are disabled. Keys such as
[Shift] and [Control] are not treated as modifier keys but are like any
other keys.

* Hybrid: It can produce text like short character mode, although without
IME and some of those features, and without control characters; keys other
than those producing text characters work like game mode.

(Events for joypad buttons (including release events) are possible in any
mode, not only game mode.)

Table of key codes:
 <0000>-<00FF>  Characters in the current code page
 <0100>-<011F>  ASCII control characters
 <0120>-<017E>  ASCII graphic characters
 <017F>         ASCII delete
 <0200>-<02FF>  Numbered function keys (some use inherent modifiers)

Modifier bits:

* bit16 = Combinations including [Command]. These would usually bypass
the current code page, and use the ASCII range when [Command] is combined
with a ASCII or ISO 646 key.

* bit17 = Number pad keys. For text entry with IME enabled, this bit would
not be used, but it can be meaningful in other circumstances.

* bit18 = Combinations including [System]. These combinations are not
usually visible to application programs, but some of them may be exposed
to the window manager.

Notes about specific key codes:

* ASCII graphic characters: In command mode, and in combinations that use
the [Command] key, these may represent ISO 646 in some keyboard layouts.

* [Begin Copy], [End Copy], [Cancel Copy]: These are used when multiple
text characters will be sent at once, either from the IME or from being
copied from another window or from keyboard input automation, etc. In such
a case, it will be bracketed by [Begin Copy] and [End Copy], with [Cancel
Copy] being used if the copy should be cancelled. This might be used to
avoid unnecessarily redrawing the screen, to avoid automatically executing
commands being copied (for security), etc. However, in most cases, this is
not needed and the objects can be copied directly, but if that is not
possible (or not desirable) for whatever reason, then this can be used.

* [IME Begin], [IME Commit], [IME Restart], [IME Cancel], [IME Substitute]:
Some IME implementations might use these, to indicate parts of the text
written by IME that has not yet been committed and might be changed. [IME
Substitute] is like [IME Cancel] followed by [Begin Copy], and [IME
Restart] is like [IME Cancel] followed by [IME Begin].

